{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There are leftover temp dirs from my test runs. The `home` temp dirs contain `go/pkg/mod` (the Go module cache). This means the `go run` command set GOMODCACHE or GOPATH to `$HOME/go/pkg/mod`? No wait — actually the deploy program's `go run` uses `HOME` env var. When I set `HOME=home` in the deploy env, the Go toolchain uses `$HOME/go/pkg/mod` as the module cache (GOPATH defaults to `$HOME/go`).\n\nWait, but the deploy program is compiled/run with `go run .` in prog_dir. The `go run` command itself is invoked via `run([go, 'run', '.', REPO_ROOT], cwd=prog_dir, env=env)` where env has `HOME=home`. So the Go toolchain downloads the toolchain to `$HOME/go/pkg/mod` (since the go.mod may specify a toolchain version like go1.25.0).\n\nActually, this is why there's a `go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.0` — the go.mod likely specifies `toolchain go1.25.0` which Go auto-downloads to `$HOME/go/pkg/mod` when HOME is set to the temp dir.\n\nWait, actually, this is a side effect of setting HOME to a temp dir. The `go run` with a fresh HOME causes Go to re-download the toolchain and module cache. This is wasteful and creates large temp dirs.\n\nBut actually, the original task9.py did the same thing (`env['HOME'] = home`). Let me check — maybe task9.py had the same issue but it's \"acceptable\" since temp dirs are cleaned by the OS eventually.\n\nActually, the issue is my `shutil.rmtree(home, ignore_errors=True)` didn't clean these up. Wait, why? Let me look again.\n\nOh wait — I see. The `agent_dir` rmtree and `home` rmtree are both called. But the output shows TWO leftover home dirs:\n- `/tmp/rick_task12_home_6vp6d7af` (from first run)\n- `/tmp/rick_task12_home_ktji8jru` (from second run)\n\nAnd `agent_dir` dirs are cleaned (no `/tmp/rick_task12_agent_*` leftover).\n\nSo the `home` dirs are NOT being cleaned. Why? Let me look at my code again:\n\n```python\n    agent_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='rick_task12_agent_')\n    home = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='rick_task12_home_')\n    env = os.environ.copy()\n    env['RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR'] = agent_dir\n    env['HOME'] = home\n    rc, out, err = run([go, 'run', '.', REPO_ROOT], cwd=prog_dir, timeout=240, env=env)\n    if rc != 0:\n        errors.append(...)\n    else:\n        ...\n    shutil.rmtree(agent_dir, ignore_errors=True)\n    shutil.rmtree(home, ignore_errors=True)\n```\n\nThe `shutil.rmtree(home, ignore_errors=True)` should clean it. But the home dir still exists with go/pkg/mod content.\n\nHmm wait — maybe the rmtree happened but the dir was recreated? No. Or maybe the `go run` process is still running in the background writing to home? No, run() waits for completion.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The `go run .` uses `cwd=prog_dir`. The `env` has `HOME=home`. When Go runs, it uses `$HOME/go/pkg/mod` as GOMODCACHE. It downloads the toolchain there.\n\nAfter `go run` finishes, `shutil.rmtree(home, ignore_errors=True)` should remove it. But it's still there with content.\n\nLet me check: maybe the rmtree IS being called but the content is being written by a concurrent process. No.\n\nActually, wait. Let me look more carefully. Is it possible that `shutil.rmtree(home)` failed silently due to `ignore_errors=True`? What could cause rmtree to fail? Permission issues? The dirs have `drwx------` (0700) and `drwxrwxr-x` (0775) permissions.\n\nActually, let me just check if there's a file lock or something. Let me manually try to remove it.\n\nActually, the more likely explanation: The `go run` command with `HOME=home` triggers Go to download the toolchain into `$HOME/go`. But this download might still be in progress when... no, subprocess.run waits.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the sequence. Actually there might be TWO `go run` invocations or the `go` toolchain spawns background processes.\n\nActually, let me just check: is the leftover from a run that happened BEFORE my rmtree, or is rmtree actually failing?\n\nLet me manually test removing it."}}
