his is the case here — tasks 1-11 done), OR rewrite if feature NOT implemented but test passes (weak assertion).\n\nSince tasks 1-11 are all \"success\" (committed), and I verified each assertion maps to real, non-trivial checks (files actually contain the keywords, dirs actually exist, deploy actually runs and produces files, commands actually exit 0), the test passing is the \"acceptable\" case.\n\nBut let me do a quick sanity check that the test CAN fail, to be rigorous about TDD. I'll do it non-destructively: create a temporary redundant directory, run just the O2 check logic, confirm it errors, then remove it. Actually, that's a bit risky. \n\nAlternatively, I can verify the assertion logic in isolation using a Python one-liner that imports the test's functions. Let me do a lightweight verification: run a small script that monkeypatches/checks the detection.\n\nActually, the simplest safe RED verification: temporarily add a redundant dir `internal/executor` (empty), run the test, confirm it fails with the right error, then remove it. But wait — `go test ./...` or `go build ./...` might try to compile an empty package... no, an empty dir without .go files is ignored by Go. And the O2 check just checks `os.path.isdir`. So creating an empty `internal/executor/` dir would make O2 fail, and removing it restores.\n\nBut actually, creating a dir in the repo is a modification. It's temporary and I'll remove it immediately. This is a legitimate RED verification. Let me do it carefully.\n\nActually, let me instead verify the test's failure detection more cheaply using a Python check that doesn't touch the repo: I'll verify that `total_matches` and `files_with_match` return the correct values, and that the baseline comparison logic is correct.\n\nLet me just do a quick verification of the key computed values to confirm the assertions aren't trivially true."}}
